<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Aquinas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1225">1225</a>–<a href="/years/1274">1274</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Aquinas was a thirteenth-century Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> and theologian, the most influential philosopher of medieval scholasticism. His synthesis of Christian theology and Aristotelian philosophy drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and of commentaries by Muslim thinkers. The commentaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a> (Averroes), written under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> patronage, influenced Aquinas and the course of medieval Western thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas.jpg" alt="Thomas Aquinas" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlo Crivelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St-thomas-aquinasFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Averroes, working under Almohad patronage, produced commentaries on Aristotle that transformed European scholastic philosophy when transmitted through Latin translation, influencing <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> and the wider course of medieval Western thought.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The University of Paris made the city the centre of scholastic theology, where <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> taught in the thirteenth century, and Louis IX, canonised in 1297, became a model of Christian kingship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Medieval theology culminated in the work of <b>Thomas Aquinas</b>, whose Summa Theologiae united Christian doctrine with the philosophy of Aristotle.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Middle Ages Latin carried philosophy, history, poetry and science: Bede’s history of the English Church, the hymns of the liturgy, the poems of the wandering scholars and the works of <b>Thomas Aquinas</b> were all written in Latin.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Aquinas</b> taught that a king must govern according to natural law and respect the customs of his people.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Aquinas</b> commended a mixed regime combining kingship with rule by the best and by the many, and Sir John Fortescue, writing around 1470, contrasted England, a “political and royal” lordship whose king could neither change the laws nor tax without his subjects’ assent, with the purely royal…</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
